WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.061
A practical threat hunting workflow starts with a defensive question.

00:00:04.061 --> 00:00:09.607
What behavior are you looking for, and why does it matter to the environment?

00:00:09.607 --> 00:00:15.549
The question should be narrow enough to investigate and broad enough to produce useful learning.

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:21.094
From there, define scope: business unit, asset group, identity set, cloud account, time window,

00:00:21.094 --> 00:00:22.283
and data sources.

00:00:22.283 --> 00:00:26.244
Scope keeps the work bounded and makes conclusions more honest.

00:00:26.244 --> 00:00:27.828
Next, write a hypothesis.

00:00:27.828 --> 00:00:32.582
A good hypothesis names suspected behavior, affected assets or identities, required telemetry,

00:00:32.582 --> 00:00:34.562
expected observations, and decision criteria.

00:00:34.562 --> 00:00:36.147
It should be testable.

00:00:36.147 --> 00:00:38.920
It should also be allowed to fail.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:44.861
A rejected hypothesis can still be valuable if it was tested carefully and documented clearly.

00:00:44.861 --> 00:00:46.050
Then identify telemetry.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:49.615
Decide which endpoint, identity, DNS, proxy, firewall, EDR, cloud,

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:52.388
or email data should show the behavior.

00:00:52.388 --> 00:00:56.349
Check retention, field quality, timestamps, host naming, and known gaps.

00:00:56.349 --> 00:01:01.895
If the data cannot answer the question, document that limitation and refine the hunt.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:03.479
Query and pivot deliberately.

00:01:03.479 --> 00:01:06.648
Start with the behavior, not a random search.

00:01:06.648 --> 00:01:11.797
Pivot from host to user, user to source IP, process to parent process,

00:01:11.797 --> 00:01:14.570
domain to DNS clients, hash to endpoints,

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.324
or alert to surrounding timeline when the pivot helps test the hypothesis.

00:01:19.324 --> 00:01:24.077
Reduce noise, but do not filter so aggressively that the signal disappears.

00:01:24.077 --> 00:01:26.850
Save queries, assumptions, exclusions, and time windows.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.038
Validate before concluding.

00:01:28.038 --> 00:01:31.603
Compare against baselines, asset roles, user context, business explanations,

00:01:31.603 --> 00:01:32.792
and related events.

00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:37.545
Use careful language: unusual, suspicious, malicious, and confirmed are not the same.

00:01:37.545 --> 00:01:41.902
Escalate to incident response when the evidence meets your organization's criteria,

00:01:41.902 --> 00:01:45.468
and preserve the evidence needed for responders to act.

00:01:45.468 --> 00:01:48.240
Finally, document the outcome and improve defenses.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.390
A hunt can produce new detection logic, tuned alerts, improved logging, updated playbooks,

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.559
documented gaps, asset context, or threat intelligence feedback.

00:01:56.559 --> 00:02:00.124
Threat hunting is not random searching or alert chasing.

00:02:00.124 --> 00:02:06.066
It is structured investigation that turns questions into evidence and evidence into stronger security operations.

00:02:06.066 --> 00:02:09.235
For a beginner, the checklist is intentionally simple.

00:02:09.235 --> 00:02:12.800
Question, scope, hypothesis, telemetry, query, pivot, validate, document, improve.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.365
If a hunt feels confusing, return to that sequence.

00:02:16.365 --> 00:02:21.514
The workflow does not remove analyst judgment, but it gives judgment a structure.

00:02:21.514 --> 00:02:22.307
Over time,

00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:27.060
that structure helps a SOC turn individual curiosity into repeatable defensive capability.
